  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "Introduce the Lucas numbers in Lean and express them through the shallow diagonals of Pascal's triangle, together with a mixed Fibonacci–Lucas identity, reusing the parent's Fibonacci diagonal sum.",
    "plain": "Answers the parent's second open question: the companion Lucas-number diagonal identities and a mixed Fibonacci–Lucas identity. Mathlib has Fibonacci numbers but no Lucas sequence, so this entry introduces the Lucas numbers L(0)=2, L(1)=1, L(n+2)=L(n)+L(n+1) and proves (i) the bridge L(n)+F(n)=2·F(n+1), (ii) the Lucas–Fibonacci relation L(n+1)=F(n+2)+F(n), (iii) that L(n+2) is the sum of two consecutive shallow diagonals of Pascal's triangle (in both index conventions), reducing to the parent's fib_shallow_diagonal, and (iv) the mixed product identity F(2n)=F(n)·L(n) via Mathlib's Nat.fib_two_mul. Axiom-free.",
    "whyMatters": []
  },
  "knownResults": {
    "proven": [
      "lucas: the Lucas sequence L(0)=2, L(1)=1, L(n+2)=L(n)+L(n+1), absent from Mathlib (which has only the unrelated Lucas–Lehmer primality test)",
      "lucas_add_fib: the subtraction-free bridge L(n) + F(n) = 2·F(n+1)",
      "lucas_eq: the Lucas–Fibonacci relation L(n+1) = F(n+2) + F(n)",
      "lucas_shallow_diagonal: L(n+2) = ∑_j C(n+2−j, j) + ∑_j C(n−j, j), the companion Lucas diagonal identity (sum of two consecutive shallow diagonals of Pascal's triangle)",
      "lucas_shallow_diagonal_alt: the mirror form L(n+2) = ∑_k C(k, n+2−k) + ∑_k C(k, n−k)",
      "fib_two_mul_lucas: the mixed Fibonacci–Lucas product identity F(2n) = F(n)·L(n)"
    ],
